Bluetooth: hci_bcm: accept a threaded host-wake interrupt

On the S5L8947X board the BCM4334 host-wake line goes to a GPIO input of
the I2C power management unit, so the interrupt comes from a regmap-irq
chip whose interrupts are nested in its threaded handler. Such an
interrupt cannot take a hard-IRQ-only handler: devm_request_irq() fails,
hci_bcm then leaves runtime PM and the controller's UART sleep mode off,
and nothing reports why.

Request it with devm_request_any_context_irq(), which uses a threaded
handler where the interrupt is nested. bcm_host_wake() only takes and
drops a runtime PM reference, so it is safe in either context.
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c | 13 +++++++------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
@@ -348,14 +348,15 @@
 		goto unlock;
 	}

-	err = devm_request_irq(bdev->dev, bdev->irq, bcm_host_wake,
-			       bdev->irq_active_low ? IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING :
-						      IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING,
-			       "host_wake", bdev);
-	if (err) {
+	err = devm_request_any_context_irq(bdev->dev, bdev->irq, bcm_host_wake,
+					   bdev->irq_active_low ? IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING :
+								  IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING,
+					   "host_wake", bdev);
+	if (err < 0) {
 		bdev->irq = err;
 		goto unlock;
 	}
+	err = 0;

 	bdev->irq_acquired = true;

